switch is for hosts to communicate with each other within the same network, one switch is one network
$ ip link    //list or modify interfaces on the host
if switch ip address is 192.168.1.0, we can assign the hosts within this network as below:
$ ip addr add 192.168.1.10/24 dev eth0     //to set ip addresses on the interfaces
$ ip addr add 192.168.1.11/24 dev eth0     //to set ip addresses on the interfaces
so computers can now coomunciate with each other through the switch in this way:
$ ping 192.168.1.11

routing is for hosts communicate with each other between different networks
say there's another switch which ip address is 192.168.2.0, we use a router to connect these two networks, the router gets two ip assigned, one on each network
192.168.1.1 and 192.168.2.1 
if 192.168.1.11 wants to commnicate with 192.168.2.11, it needs to go through the route/gateway, the gateway is the door to other networks or to Internet 
to see the system routing config, run:
$ route    //nothing shows up for now
to config the gateway on system B (192.168.1.11) to reach systems on network 2.0 (192.168.2.0), run:
$ ip route add 192.168.2.0/24 via 192.168.1.1      //to add entries into the routing table, in this command front part is destination
$ route    //run this command again it would show up the config now
say you need access to Google route 172.217.194.0
$ ip route add 172.217.194.0 via 192.168.2.1
to access to any other internet that you didnt config in your system:
$ ip route add default via 192.168.2.1    //$ ip route add 0.0.0.0 via 192.168.2.1

ip addr    //to see the ip addresses assigned to the interfaces

ip route     //to see routing table

cat /proc/sys/net/ipv4/ip_forward      //to check if ip forward is enabled on a host if you are working on a host as a router

